WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.313
A secure SignalR baseline starts with the
right mental model.

2
00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:10.947
SignalR is a real-time communication
framework, not a trust boundary.

3
00:00:11.027 --> 00:00:15.933
Hubs, methods, messages, users, groups,
browser transports,

4
00:00:16.013 --> 00:00:22.677
and connection lifecycle decisions are all
part of the application design and should

5
00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:24.886
be reviewed deliberately.

6
00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:30.520
Authenticate connections explicitly, then
authorize hubs, methods,

7
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.470
and sensitive actions on the server.

8
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:39.566
Do not rely on UI hiding or group
membership as the complete permission model.

9
00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:45.478
Validate hub method arguments and message
content, use narrow contracts,

10
00:00:45.558 --> 00:00:51.574
and verify client-supplied identifiers
against trusted server-side data.

11
00:00:51.654 --> 00:00:56.930
Protect tokens, connection data, and
browser transport behavior.

12
00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:02.749
Review CORS, WebSocket origins, HTTPS,
query-string logging, proxies,

13
00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:07.550
load balancers, scale-out, and production
configuration.

14
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:13.739
Constrain message size, streaming
behavior, reconnect buffering, fan-out,

15
00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:19.558
and high-frequency events so the real-time
system remains predictable.

16
00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:24.914
Finally, return safe errors and log useful
events without secrets.

17
00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:30.363
A mature SignalR application is
observable, bounded, and governed.

18
00:01:30.443 --> 00:01:36.367
It gives users real-time behavior while
keeping identity, authorization,

19
00:01:36.447 --> 00:01:42.741
validation, resource control, and
deployment review firmly under server-side

20
00:01:42.821 --> 00:01:44.580
engineering control.
